          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Budget folders and walking pads</h3>
          <p>
            When a new NordicTrack T Series 5 costs $445.75 and walking pads sell for under $150 on Amazon, there is very
            little room for a used one to undercut new, and these machines have the shortest lives in the table above.
            A used budget folder is worth buying only if it is nearly free and you can carry it home yourself.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Mid-range NordicTrack, Sole, Horizon and Bowflex</h3>
          <p>
            This is where used buying can work, because the frames and motors last. Take a used Sole F80 as the worked
            example. New, it was $1,499.99 at Sole on 23 November. If the used one needs a $139.99 belt and $10 of
            lubricant, and you would pay a mover $200 to collect it, the used machine is already costing you about $350
            before you have paid the seller, with no warranty. A sensible offer has to sit far enough below $1,150 to pay
            for that risk; how far is your call, but it is not the &ldquo;half of what I paid&rdquo; the seller may have in mind
            if they paid list price years ago. The $200 moving figure is an illustration: get a real quote for your stairs
            and distance.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Peloton</h3>
          <p>
            A used original Tread at the $1,800 typical sale price, plus $95 activation and $500 to $900 delivery, comes to
            about $2,395 to $2,795 delivered, against $1,995 for a new Tread Flex this week. Buying on Repowered removes the
            $95, and collecting it yourself removes the delivery, which brings it to about $1,800 with no warranty. Our <a href="/how-much-does-a-peloton-tread-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton Tread cost guide</a> has the full ownership sums.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="new-on-sale" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>When New on Sale Beats Used</h2>
<p>
            Black Friday is on Friday 27 November and Cyber Monday on 30 November, and this is the week when a used
            treadmill has the hardest job. Use these rules of thumb, built on the prices above.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Under about $1,000 new, buy new on sale.</strong> With the Horizon T101 at $784.40 and the Bowflex T6
            at $899 this week, each with a warranty and delivery, a used budget machine saves too little to cover a belt
            and a move.</li>
            <li><strong>Peloton: compare against the sale price.</strong> The used Tread&rsquo;s $1,800 typical sale is only
            $195 under the $1,995 Tread Flex before delivery and activation, so used wins only if you collect it yourself
            and buy through Repowered or negotiate well below that figure.</li>
            <li><strong>Mid-range: used can win, but only collected.</strong> A three-to-five-year-old Sole, Horizon or
            NordicTrack Commercial that was lubricated and can be collected by you is where the real savings are,
            provided parts are still sold.</li>
            <li><strong>Commercial: buy remanufactured, not private.</strong> A rebuilt club treadmill at $3,799 or more with
            a 1-year parts and labour warranty is a sound buy for heavy users; a private-sale ex-gym machine with no
            history is a gamble on the most expensive parts.</li>
            <li><strong>Curved trainers: look for maker seconds.</strong> With slat belts and no motor, a curved trainer is a
            better used bet than a motorised one, and SB Fitness&rsquo;s seconds were about 13% off new.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The sale prices shift daily this week. Our <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday treadmill deals</a> page and <a href="/black-friday-treadmill-deals-under-1000/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">deals under
            $1,000</a> track them, and <a href="/cyber-monday-treadmill-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Cyber
            Monday deals</a> follow on. Check the new price on the day you make an offer on a used machine; it is the most
            persuasive number in any negotiation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="negotiating" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Negotiating and Paying Safely</h2>
<p>
            Once you have a ceiling, the inspection is what earns you a lower price. Run the machine at walking speed, at
            running speed and through the full incline range, and note anything the checklist flags: a slipping or
            frayed belt, a dry deck, grinding from the rollers, a motor that labours or a console that resets. Each is a
            specific cost you can name, with a part price from the hidden-costs table, rather than a vague request for a
            discount.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Bring the new price.</strong> Show the seller this week&rsquo;s sale price for the replacement model. It
            reframes the conversation from what they paid to what the market charges.</li>
            <li><strong>Agree who moves it.</strong> If the seller will help carry it out or has it near ground level, that is
            worth money to you; if it is in a basement with a turn at the stairs, it costs you.</li>
            <li><strong>Never pay a deposit unseen.</strong> Stock photos, a price far below comparable listings and a seller
            who cannot meet are the classic warning signs. Pay on collection, after you have run it.</li>
            <li><strong>Get the paperwork.</strong> The original order or delivery confirmation establishes the purchase date
            and ownership; for connected machines, confirm it has been removed from the seller&rsquo;s account.</li>
            <li><strong>Check for recalls</strong> on the model before you go, and confirm any remedy was fitted.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If a seller will not let you run the machine, walk away. The cost of a failed control board or motor dwarfs
            any saving, and our <a href="/how-long-do-treadmill-motors-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">motor
            lifespan guide</a> explains why a quiet, cool-running motor matters more than a clean console.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Where People Go Wrong When Pricing a Used Treadmill</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from comparing against the wrong new price.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Comparing against the original list price</h3>
              <p>A seller's "half what I paid" means nothing if the replacement model is on sale this week. Compare against today's sale price for the nearest new equivalent.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Forgetting the move</h3>
              <p>Professional delivery of a used Peloton treadmill typically runs $500 to $900, per Commonplace. Get a quote or arrange collection before agreeing a price.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming the warranty transfers</h3>
              <p>Many home warranties cover the original buyer only. Price a private-sale machine as having none unless the maker confirms otherwise in writing.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a model with no parts</h3>
              <p>Search for a belt and control board for the exact model before you travel. If none is sold, the first fault ends the machine.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">A used treadmill is worth what the nearest new machine costs on sale, minus the
            warranty, the move and the wear you inherit.</strong> In the pairs we could date this month, maker seconds sold
            at about 87% of new, remanufactured gym treadmills at roughly half to three-fifths of a new commercial price,
            and used Peloton Treads around half the list price of their replacements.
          </p>
          <p>
            In Black Friday week, new budget treadmills under $1,000 are hard for any used machine to beat. Save the used
            route for a mid-range or commercial frame you can inspect and collect, or a remanufactured machine with a
            written warranty, and run through our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">used treadmill buying checklist</a> before you pay.
          </p>
        </div>

