        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How much does a used treadmill cost?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on the seller. On 25 November 2026 remanufactured gym treadmills cost $3,799 to $10,599 at one specialist dealer, maker scratch-and-dent curved trainers sold at about 87% of new, and a resale marketplace put a typical used Peloton Tread sale at about $1,800 in August 2026. Private-sale prices vary too much to quote; price-check them against comparable listings and new sale prices.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What percentage of the new price should I pay for a used treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              There is no reliable single percentage. In the dated pairs we found, maker seconds were about 87% of new, remanufactured commercial machines 43% to 59% of a new commercial price, and used Peloton treadmills roughly half the list price of their replacements. For a private sale, start from the new sale price and subtract moving, needed parts and the missing warranty.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much is a used Peloton Tread worth?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Commonplace, a resale marketplace, reported a typical used Tread sale of about $1,800 (median $1,786) in August 2026 and a median asking price of $2,157.50 on 7 October. A new Tread Flex was $1,995 at Peloton on 24 November 2026, so a used Tread needs to sit clearly below that once activation and delivery are added.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are refurbished treadmills worth it?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              They can be, when the refurbisher states what was replaced and gives a written warranty. Fitness Superstore, for example, includes a 1-year parts and on-site labour warranty on its remanufactured treadmills. Compare the price against a new machine on sale, not against its list price.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How many hours is too many on a used treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Our checklist treats under 500 hours on a home machine as light use and over 1,500 hours as approaching mid-life; commercial machines are built for far more. Maintenance matters more than hours, so ask when the deck was last lubricated.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it cheaper to buy a used treadmill or a new one on Black Friday?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Below about $1,000 new, the Black Friday price usually wins once you count delivery, a likely belt and the warranty you would lose. Used makes more sense for mid-range and commercial machines you can collect yourself, or for remanufactured machines sold with a warranty.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
